  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>first option</b> is correct because it details a scalable, automated process using AWS Data Pipeline to transfer data from DynamoDB to S3 each day. This solution not only makes the data readily available for your product manager without manual work, but it also supports the growing nature of your dataset as the number of items increases daily. The S3 files can be easily accessed, analyzed, and integrated with various reporting tools.</p> <p>The <i>second option</i> incorrectly recommends manually exporting the table via the console each day. While this might work for very small datasets or short periods, it's inefficient and error-prone as the data grows, and does not fit operational best practices for automation.<br/>The <i>third option</i> uses a Data Pipeline but specifies running it across a multi-hour window (from 8 AM to 8 PM) rather than simply as a daily job. This would create unnecessary complexity, as you only need to export the data once per day for reporting; continuous runs aren't required for a once-a-day snapshot.<br/>The <i>fourth option</i> is redundant as it essentially repeats part of the correct answer, but it is less complete and doesn't clearly indicate automation by specifying a suitable time.<br/><b>Tip:</b> For recurring processes involving data growth and daily reporting needs, always look for ways to automate regular exports using AWS services like Data Pipeline or DynamoDB Export to S3, ensuring scalability and minimal manual intervention.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your team is developing a function that aims to increase how often users utilize your application.<br/>You have set up a DynamoDB table to log activities, tracking how the feature contributes to greater usage rates. The table receives around 2,000 new records per day, with growth over time.<br/>Your product manager has asked to review this daily data from the DynamoDB table.<br/>What is the best way to prepare and present the daily data to the product manager?</p>",
